1
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:11,224
My name is Gaius. I served twenty-five years on the Wall, at the northern edge of the Roman Empire.

2
00:00:11,524 --> 00:00:20,366
I'm an old man now. My hands shake. But I still remember one winter better than all the rest.

3
00:00:20,466 --> 00:00:26,471
The winter of the year one hundred and thirty, when the tribes took three of our men.

4
00:00:26,571 --> 00:00:31,197
Rome went after them. I went with them.

5
00:00:31,797 --> 00:00:34,239
Not all of us came back.

6
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:39,005
If you've followed this story from the start, you know how it began.

7
00:00:39,105 --> 00:00:48,843
Four scouts went missing beyond the Wall. Three lights came out of the fog. I blew the horn, and the whole Wall woke up.

8
00:00:48,943 --> 00:00:54,245
In the morning, we found my friend Felix alive, in a shepherd's hut.

9
00:00:54,345 --> 00:00:59,664
And he told us the other three were still out there. Prisoners.

10
00:01:09,114 --> 00:01:13,939
The order came from the fort at Vindolanda the next afternoon.

11
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:22,688
Find the prisoners. Bring them back. And show the tribes what it costs to take a Roman soldier.

12
00:01:22,788 --> 00:01:29,423
Our centurion picked thirty men. The cavalry commander picked thirty riders.

13
00:01:29,523 --> 00:01:34,144
And Felix, who could barely stand, said he was coming too.

14
00:01:34,244 --> 00:01:41,129
He was the only one of us who had seen where they were taken. Nobody could argue with that.

15
00:01:41,229 --> 00:01:52,505
I still remember the names of the three prisoners. Titus, a quiet man from Gaul. Verus, who was always in trouble. And Lucco.

16
00:01:52,605 --> 00:02:04,556
Lucco looked after the pigs. I'm not joking. A real wooden tablet from Vindolanda mentions a soldier called Lucco, in charge of pigs.

17
00:02:04,656 --> 00:02:14,536
Maybe it was our Lucco. Maybe not. But that's how I remember him. Pigs, and a terrible singing voice.

18
00:02:14,636 --> 00:02:22,774
Rufus volunteered too. Rufus, who slept through the alarm. He said he owed us one.

19
00:02:26,924 --> 00:02:34,064
We went out through the north gate at dawn. Sixty men. Nobody looked back.

20
00:02:34,164 --> 00:02:42,819
North of the Wall there were no roads. Just moor, and bog, and low hills, and rain that never quite stopped.

21
00:02:42,919 --> 00:02:47,835
Felix rode in front, wrapped in his sheepskin, pointing the way.

22
00:02:47,935 --> 00:02:52,034
Every evening, wherever we stopped, we built a camp.

23
00:02:52,134 --> 00:03:00,153
That's what Roman soldiers did. Every night, even for one night, even in enemy country, you dig.

24
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:11,642
A ditch all the way around. The earth thrown up into a bank. A fence of wooden stakes on top. Tents in straight rows inside.

25
00:03:11,742 --> 00:03:20,323
It takes about three hours. You do it after marching all day. And you complain the whole time.

26
00:03:20,423 --> 00:03:28,874
Archaeologists can still see the outlines of these camps from the air, all over Scotland. Hundreds of them.

27
00:03:28,974 --> 00:03:34,344
That night, Rufus asked Felix what the tribes would do to the prisoners.

28
00:03:34,444 --> 00:03:41,879
Felix didn't answer for a long time. Then he said: if we're quick, nothing.

29
00:03:42,379 --> 00:03:44,504
Nobody slept much.

30
00:03:48,654 --> 00:03:51,346
On the third day, we saw it.

31
00:03:51,446 --> 00:04:01,933
A flat-topped hill, standing alone above the moor. Rings of earth and stone around the top. Smoke rising from inside.

32
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:09,655
A hillfort. The tribes had used places like this for centuries, long before Rome came.

33
00:04:09,755 --> 00:04:19,011
We counted the fires. More than twenty. Maybe two hundred people up there. Far more than us.

34
00:04:19,111 --> 00:04:24,141
And somewhere inside, in a pen like animals, our three friends.

35
00:04:24,241 --> 00:04:33,713
There's a real hill in the south of Scotland called Burnswark. It has a hillfort on top, and two Roman camps at its foot.

36
00:04:33,813 --> 00:04:41,067
Archaeologists have found hundreds of lead sling bullets there, scattered across the old ramparts.

37
00:04:41,167 --> 00:04:48,710
Nobody is sure exactly what happened at Burnswark. But it looks a lot like what we did.

38
00:04:48,810 --> 00:04:58,072
Our centurion had two choices. Attack up that hill in daylight, and lose half his men. Or wait for dark.

39
00:04:58,872 --> 00:05:02,943
What would you have done? Tell me in the comments.

40
00:05:03,543 --> 00:05:05,668
He waited for dark.

41
00:05:09,818 --> 00:05:15,556
The plan was simple. Most plans that get men killed are.

42
00:05:15,656 --> 00:05:25,400
The cavalry would wait below, at the ford. The rest of the century would make noise at the main gate, with torches and trumpets.

43
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:33,389
And while every eye was on that gate, six of us would go up the back of the hill, by a sheep path Felix had seen.

44
00:05:33,489 --> 00:05:44,560
Felix, Rufus, me, and three others. No armour. No shields. Just swords, and knives, and dark cloaks.

45
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:48,728
I've never been so frightened in my life.

46
00:05:48,828 --> 00:05:56,235
The path was steep and slippery. Somewhere above us, a dog barked, and we all froze.

47
00:05:56,635 --> 00:06:00,081
It stopped. We kept climbing.

48
00:06:00,181 --> 00:06:03,985
Then, from the far side of the hill, our trumpets.

49
00:06:04,085 --> 00:06:13,188
Torches. Shouting. The whole fort ran to the main gate, just as the centurion said they would.

50
00:06:13,588 --> 00:06:16,938
We went over the back wall in the dark.

51
00:06:17,038 --> 00:06:25,284
The pen was where Felix said it would be. A ring of woven branches, and a guard asleep against it.

52
00:06:25,884 --> 00:06:29,075
I won't tell you what happened to the guard.

53
00:06:29,175 --> 00:06:36,269
Inside were two men, tied hand and foot. Titus. And Verus.

54
00:06:36,769 --> 00:06:42,088
I asked where Lucco was. Verus just shook his head.

55
00:06:42,888 --> 00:06:48,320
Lucco had tried to run on the first night. He didn't make it.

56
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:55,253
We cut them loose, and we ran.

57
00:06:55,353 --> 00:06:59,361
We almost reached the back wall before someone saw us.

58
00:06:59,561 --> 00:07:06,281
A shout. Then a hundred shouts. Then the air was full of stones.

59
00:07:06,381 --> 00:07:14,514
The tribes were deadly with slings. A stone from a sling can break a man's skull at a hundred paces.

60
00:07:14,614 --> 00:07:22,270
Something hit Rufus in the back, and he went down. Felix and I dragged him over the rampart.

61
00:07:22,370 --> 00:07:28,943
Then we were falling, sliding down the hill in the dark, with the whole hillside coming after us.

62
00:07:29,043 --> 00:07:35,565
At the bottom was the ford. And at the ford, thirty horsemen in a line, waiting.

63
00:07:35,865 --> 00:07:44,588
I'll never forget that sound. Thirty horses charging through the water, straight past us, into the men behind.

64
00:07:44,688 --> 00:07:52,826
The tribes broke, and ran back up the hill. The cavalry didn't follow. They didn't have to.

65
00:07:53,626 --> 00:07:59,994
We were across the river. We were alive. All of us, except Lucco.

66
00:08:04,144 --> 00:08:10,348
We came back to the Wall three days later. Slowly, because of Rufus.

67
00:08:10,448 --> 00:08:19,120
His back was black and blue, but nothing was broken. He complained all the way home. We let him.

68
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:24,340
The whole milecastle was up on the walkway to watch us come in.

69
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:29,906
Somebody had brewed beer. Felix finally got his cup.

70
00:08:30,006 --> 00:08:36,329
That night, the centurion wrote his report, on a wooden tablet, like all the others.

71
00:08:36,429 --> 00:08:46,082
Two soldiers recovered. One soldier dead. One wounded. A war band driven off.

72
00:08:46,182 --> 00:08:54,598
Just a few lines. If it had survived in the mud at Vindolanda, it would look like every other report.

73
00:08:54,698 --> 00:09:03,710
That's the thing about history. Most of it is lost. A few lines on a piece of wood, if you're lucky.

74
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:11,330
But behind every line, there were people. Frightened, tired, cold, and very, very brave.

75
00:09:11,430 --> 00:09:16,323
We built Lucco a cairn of stones on the crags, looking north.

76
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:23,253
Felix sang at it. Badly. In Lucco's honour.

77
00:09:27,403 --> 00:09:31,321
I stayed on the Wall for seventeen more years.

78
00:09:31,421 --> 00:09:38,215
Felix and I served together the whole time. He snored the whole time, too.

79
00:09:38,315 --> 00:09:48,632
When my twenty-five years were done, Rome gave me my discharge, a payment in silver, and I bought a little farm, just south of the Wall.

80
00:09:48,732 --> 00:10:03,792
Most old soldiers did the same. They didn't go home to Italy, or Spain, or Syria. They stayed. They married local women. Their children were British.

81
00:10:03,892 --> 00:10:11,276
The Wall was built to keep Rome and the Britons apart. In the end, it mixed them together.

82
00:10:11,376 --> 00:10:18,811
About fifty years after that winter, the tribes did cross the Wall, just as we always feared.

83
00:10:19,211 --> 00:10:26,329
By then I was a very old man on a farm, and my grandsons were soldiers themselves.

84
00:10:26,429 --> 00:10:33,927
In the end, Rome left Britain. By the year four hundred and ten, the legions were gone.

85
00:10:34,027 --> 00:10:43,288
But the Wall stayed. It's still there today. Seventy-three miles of it, across the neck of Britain.

86
00:10:43,388 --> 00:10:47,828
If you ever walk along it on a foggy night, look north.

87
00:10:48,328 --> 00:10:52,314
And if you see a light out there in the fog, don't worry.

88
00:10:53,114 --> 00:10:56,850
Somebody on the Wall is already watching it.

89
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:03,770
My name is Gaius. This was my day on the Wall.
